<%# ONE SUGGESTION: its sentence, and the control that acts on it. Local: `suggestion`
    (SuggestionEngine::Suggestion).

    The sentence is per kind (§8's table is the copy target) and lives in its own partial; what is
    shared is the frame, the accept control and the clause every PROPOSING kind carries — what
    accepting does to the category. It is here rather than in each kind's partial precisely so a
    bill and a rate cannot come to describe the same act in two different ways. (A second clause
    named the cap the acceptance would destroy; it is deleted with the cap.)

    `presenter` LEFT THE LOCALS with `#joined_pool` (two-ledger spec §5): the effect clause asked
    the page which envelope an acceptance would join, and the engine states what accepting does on
    the suggestion itself now, so this partial takes exactly what it renders.

    `data-suggestion` is kind AND subject id: a category and a pool may share a name, and
    `Capybara.exact` is unset, so a name-keyed row is one rename away from matching two. %>
<article class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0" data-suggestion="<%= suggestion_key(suggestion) %>">
  <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-start sm:justify-between gap-3">
    <div class="text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-1">
      <%= render "budget_page/suggestion_#{suggestion.kind}", suggestion: suggestion %>

      <%# WHAT ACCEPTING DOES, SAID BEFORE THE CLICK.

          THE RE-POINT SENTENCE IS DELETED AND THIS IS WHAT REPLACES IT (two-ledger spec §3/§4).
          Accepting used to move the CATEGORY onto an envelope — "puts all Utilities spending in a
          new Utilities envelope" / "joins your existing Utilities envelope" — because a rule was
          owned by a pool and money only reached one through `categories.pool_id`. There is no
          envelope and nothing is re-pointed: the category holds the money itself, and the whole of
          what accepting changes besides writing the rule is `funded_since`.

          THAT IS STILL WORTH A SENTENCE, and the same one the deleted clause was really about:
          which side of the start-date rule the user's spending falls on. From `funded_since`
          onward an entry drains the CATEGORY; before it, the entry drains what is available. So a
          category that has never held money is being started here, and one that already holds it is
          not — which is the state the SECOND rule in a category is in, and the honest replacement
          for the old "joins the envelope you already have".

          `detail[:starts_holding]` is the engine's own answer (it reads `funded_since`, which is
          exactly what `BudgetProposal` stamps), so the row and the write cannot disagree. %>
      <% if suggestion_proposes_a_rule?(suggestion) %>
        <% subject_name = suggestion.detail[:category_name] || suggestion.subject.name %>
        <p class="text-gray-500" data-suggestion-effect>
          <% if suggestion.detail[:starts_holding] %>
            Accepting this starts <%= subject_name %> counting its own spending, from today onward —
            spending before today is money already gone.
          <% else %>
            <%= subject_name %> already counts its own spending; this rule adds to what it claims.
          <% end %>
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <div class="shrink-0 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
      <%= link_to suggestion_accept_label(suggestion),
                  suggestion_accept_path(suggestion),
                  class: "btn btn-primary whitespace-nowrap",
                  data: { suggestion_accept: true } %>

      <%# PUTTING THIS ONE DOWN (Henry's ruling of 2026-08-20). On EVERY kind, deliberately: the
          two kinds about an existing rule are the ones a user is most likely to have already
          decided about — a dead rule they mean to keep, a drift they are riding out — and a Hide
          offered only on the proposing kinds would leave exactly those two nagging.

          THE PAIR THE ENGINE KEYS ON goes on the wire — kind, subject type, subject id — and not
          a rendered id of the suggestion, because a suggestion has no row and therefore no id.
          `subject_type` is checked against `SuggestionDismissal::SUBJECT_TYPES` before the
          controller looks anything up; see SuggestionDismissalsController for why a class name off
          the wire is not constantized. %>
      <%= button_to "Hide",
                    suggestion_dismissals_path,
                    params: {
                      kind: suggestion.kind,
                      subject_type: suggestion.subject.class.name,
                      subject_id: suggestion.subject.id
                    },
                    class: "btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap",
                    form: { data: { suggestion_hide: suggestion_key(suggestion) } } %>

      <% if suggestion.kind == :dead_rule %>
        <%# THE PAGE DOES NOT DELETE — the user does. Both doors are open on the row because the
            decision is genuinely theirs: a bill that stopped because the subscription was
            cancelled wants the rule gone, and one that stopped because the card was replaced wants
            it kept. Offering only "review" would hide the answer most dead rules deserve; deleting
            on "accept" would take a rule off a money screen on one click. %>
        <%= button_to "Delete the rule",
                      budget_path(suggestion.subject),
                      method: :delete,
                      class: "btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap",
                      form: { data: { turbo_confirm: "Delete this rule? Nothing else about #{suggestion.detail[:item_name]} changes." } } %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>
